{"qid":"Q191324","names":["Middle Kingdom of Egypt"],"title":"Middle Kingdom of Egypt","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Middle Kingdom (c. 2055–1650 BCE) was the period of reunification following the First Intermediate Period, initiated when Mentuhotep II of the Eleventh Dynasty defeated the Herakleopolitan rulers around 2055 BCE and re-centralized pharaonic authority from Thebes. Its apex came under the Twelfth Dynasty, particularly Senusret III (r. c. 1878–1840 BCE), who reorganized provincial administration, campaigned deep into Nubia establishing fortresses at Semna and Buhen, and conducted military expeditions into Canaan. The era produced some of Egypt's finest literature—the Tale of Sinuhe, the Dialogue of a Man with His Soul—and monumental funerary architecture at Dahshur and Hawara. It ended as central authority dissolved during the Thirteenth Dynasty's rapid succession of ephemeral kings, enabling the Hyksos to establish the Fifteenth Dynasty in the Delta by around 1650 BCE.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The broad territorial extent of the Middle Kingdom in the Nile Valley is well attested through excavation, textual, and monument evidence; frontier zones in Nubia and the Levant are known from campaign inscriptions, but precise border delineation remains approximate.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Middle Kingdom","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Middle-Kingdom"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Middle Kingdom of Egypt","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Middle_Kingdom_of_Egypt"},{"label":"Met Heilbrunn: Middle Kingdom Egypt","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/egkd/hd_egkd.h